ts kept position\"))\n   578\t    (testing \"the lower routes the outstanding call, not the pair\"\n   579\t      (is (not= vm/call-in-stream-key (:stream-id e))\n   580\t          \"the retry does not take the receiver's own call-in key\")\n   581\t      (is (contains? rs (:stream-id e))\n   582\t          \"it appends on the emitter's own call-in reflection\")\n   583\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-in-stream-key)))\n   584\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-out-stream-key))\n   585\t          \"the resumer's own pair is not replaced\")\n   586\t      (let [route (get rs vm/call-out-cursor-key)]\n   587\t        (is (= kept (:cursor route))\n   588\t            \"the route cell lowers as the restored wait's cursor entry\")\n   589\t        (is (not= vm/call-out-stream-key (:stream-id route))\n   590\t            \"naming the emitter's call-out, not the receiver's key\")\n   591\t        (is (contains? rs (:stream-id route))\n   592\t            \"which is attached, so the wait resolves it\")))\n   593\t    (testing \"the retried response reaches its waiter, emitter's pair\"\n   594\t      (is (vm/blocked? r1)\n   595\t          \"first round: the request is retried, the response not yet\n   596\t           read\")\n   597\t      (is (= :warmed (:dao.stream/value warm-up))\n   598\t          \"reading the warm-up freed the slot the retry needed\")\n   599\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome landed)))\n   600\t      (is (= (:datom entry) (:dao.stream/value landed))\n   601\t          \"the retained request verbatim, on the emitter's call-in\")\n   602\t      (is (vm/blocked? r2)\n   603\t          \"the migrated call resumes and the program calls again\")\n   604\t      (is (= :next (:reason (first (:wait-set r2))))\n   605\t          \"the program's own new call is the one waiting now\"))\n   606\t    (testing \"the new call flows on the receiver's own pair\"\n   607\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome b-request)))\n   608\t      (is (= [\"world\"] (apply2/request-args (:dao.stream/value b-request)))\n   609\t          \"the future call's request is on the receiver's own call-in\")\n   610\t      (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome crossed))\n   611\t          \"and nothing crossed to the emitter's call-in\"))\n   612\t    (testing \"the receiver's own bridge answers and the program halts\"\n   613\t      (is (= \"world!\" (apply2/response-ok b-response)))\n   614\t      (is (vm/halted? r3))\n   615\t      (is (= \"world!\" (vm/value r3)))\n   616\t      (is (empty? (:wait-set r3))))))\n   617\t\n   618\t\n   619\t;; =============================================================================\n   620\t;; Unsatisfied: an unpublishable cursor, a source that cannot be\n   621\t;; served, a gone reflection\n   622\t;; =============================================================================\n   623\t\n   624\t(defn- host-cursor-stream\n   625\t  \"A handle whose cursors hold a host object -- the case\n   626\t   dao.stream.remote.md S2.3 says cannot be served.\"\n   627\t  []\n   628\t  (reify stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   629\t    (descriptor\n   630\t      [_]\n   631\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   632\t       :dao.stream/identity \"host-cursor\"\n   633\t       :dao.stream/descriptor\n   634\t       {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel\n   635\t        :dao.stream/identity \"host-cursor\"}})\n   885\t(deftest ffi-sent-round-trips\n   886\t  (let [serve! (stub-serve! chan)\n   887\t        attach! (stub-attach!)\n   888\t        resources {:yin/call-out [:handle :call-out],\n   889\t                   :yin/call-out-cursor\n   890\t                   {:stream-id :yin/call-out, :cursor 5}}\n   891\t        entry {:reason :next, :call-id :call-7, :op :op/add,\n   892\t               :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id :yin/call-out-cursor},\n   893\t               :stream-id :yin/call-out}\n   894\t        lifted (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! resources entry)\n   895\t        pending (:yin.k/pending lifted)]\n   896\t    (is (= :ffi (:yin.k/reason pending)))\n   897\t    (is (= :call-7 (:yin.k/call-id pending)))\n   898\t    (is (= :op/add (:yin.k/op pending)))\n   899\t    (is (= :yin.k/c-0 (:yin.k/cell pending)))\n   900\t    (is (= 5 (get-in lifted [:yin.k/cells :yin.k/c-0 :yin.k/position]))\n   901\t        \"the response cell at its KEPT position\")\n   902\t    (let [ctx {:segment 0, :pc 9, :env {}, :stack [], :k []}\n   903\t          lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! pending\n   904\t                                            (:yin.k/cells lifted) ctx)\n   905\t          e (:entry lowered)]\n   906\t      (is (= :next (:reason e)) \"the response wait is a :next waiter\")\n   907\t      (is (= :call-7 (:call-id e)))\n   908\t      (is (= :op/add (:op e)))\n   909\t      (is (= :yin.k/c-0 (get-in e [:cursor-ref :id])))\n   910\t      (is (= 5 (get-in lowered [:resources (:yin.k/cell pending)\n   911\t                                :cursor]))))))\n   912\t\n   913\t\n   914\t(deftest ffi-retained-round-trips\n   915\t  (let [serve! (stub-serve! chan)\n   916\t        attach! (stub-attach!)\n   917\t        envelope (apply2/request :call-7 :op/add [1 2])\n   918\t        resources {vm/call-in-stream-key [:handle :call-in],\n   919\t                   vm/call-out-stream-key [:handle :call-out],\n   920\t                   vm/call-out-cursor-key\n   921\t                   {:stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key, :cursor 2}}\n   922\t        entry {:reason :put, :request-sent true, :call-id :call-7,\n   923\t               :op :op/add, :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key,\n   924\t               :datom envelope}\n   925\t        lifted (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! resources entry)\n   926\t        pending (:yin.k/pending lifted)]\n   927\t    (is (= :ffi-request (:yin.k/reason pending)))\n   928\t    (is (= :call-7 (:yin.k/call-id pending)))\n   929\t    (is (= :op/add (:yin.k/request-op pending)))\n   930\t    (is (= [1 2] (:yin.k/request-args pending)))\n   931\t    (is (= :yin.k/c-0 (:yin.k/response-cell pending))\n   932\t        \"the response route rides as a cell\")\n   933\t    (is (= 2 (get-in lifted [:yin.k/cells :yin.k/c-0 :yin.k/position]))\n   934\t        \"the route cell at the emitter's kept call-out position\")\n   935\t    (is (= #{:dao.stream.remote/v1}\n   936\t           (get-in lifted [:yin.k/requires :yin.k/cursor-profiles]))\n   937\t        \"the frame carries a cursor profile: the route is a cell\")\n   938\t    (let [ctx {:segment 0, :pc 5, :env {}, :stack [], :k []}\n   939\t          lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! pending\n   940\t                                            (:yin.k/cells lifted) ctx)\n   941\t          e (:entry lowered)\n   942\t          rs (:resources lowered)]\n   943\t      (is (= :put (:reason e)))\n   944\t      (is (true? (:request-sent e)))\n   945\t      (is (= :call-7 (:call-id e)))\n   946\t      (is (= envelope (:datom e))\n   947\t          \"the envelope rebuilt verbatim under its call id\")\n   948\t      (is (not= vm/call-in-stream-key (:stream-id e))\n   949\t          \"the retry does not take the receiver's own call-in key\")\n   950\t      (is (= [:reflection-of\n   951\t              (get-in pending [:yin.k/request :dao.stream/identity])]\n   952\t             (get rs (:stream-id e)))\n   953\t          \"it appends on the emitter's own call-in reflection\")\n   954\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-in-stream-key)))\n   955\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-out-stream-key))\n   956\t          \"the resumer's own pair is not replaced\")\n   957\t      (let [route (get rs vm/call-out-cursor-key)\n   958\t            route-id (get-in lifted [:yin.k/cells :yin.k/c-0\n   959\t                                     :yin.k/stream\n   960\t                                     :dao.stream/identity])]\n   961\t        (is (= 2 (:cursor route))\n   962\t            \"the route cell lowers as the restored wait's cursor entry\")\n   963\t        (is (= [:reflection-of route-id] (get rs (:stream-id route)))\n   964\t            \"naming the emitter's own call-out reflection\")\n   965\t        (is (not= vm/call-out-stream-key (:stream-id route)))))))\n   966\t\n   967\t\n   968\t(deftest link-request-round-trips\n   969\t  (let [serve! (stub-serve! chan)\n   970\t        attach! (stub-attach!)\n   971\t        envelope {:yin.link/id [:t0 7], :yin.link/name 'foo,\n   972\t                  :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code,\n   973\t                  :yin.link/contract \"b2\"}\n   974\t        resources {module/link-request-resource [:handle :req],\n   975\t                   module/link-response-resource [:handle :resp]}\n   976\t        entry {:reason :link-request, :link-id [:t0 7], :name 'foo,\n   977\t               :envelope envelope,\n   978\t               :request module/link-request-resource,\n   979\t               :response module/link-response-resource,\n   980\t               :cursor :dao.stream/newest}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc | sed -n '1,760p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm.ucf.remote\n     2\t  \"The UCF facade through `dao.stream.remote` descriptors: lifts a\n     3\t   parked semantic machine's pending wait\n     4\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.4.3) into\n     5\t   UCF data whose cells and stream markers name `dao.stream.remote`\n     6\t   descriptors under the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile\n     7\t   (S7.5.3), and lowers that data back into a resumable local wait\n     8\t   through a reflection (`docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md` S2.2,\n     9\t   S2.4).\n    10\t\n    11\t   The local side is the engine's own parked machinery, not an\n    12\t   invented shape. A pending entry is what `yin.vm` parks: the\n    13\t   machine registers beside `:stream-id` and `:cursor-ref`\n    14\t   (`yin.vm.semantic` S3.5's park entries, `response-wait-entry`;\n    15\t   `yin.vm.engine`'s wait-set sweep), every handle and cursor\n    16\t   resolved out of the machine's private `:resources` table -- the\n    17\t   lift reads the table, and no entry ever carries a live handle.\n    18\t   The lower mirrors it: fresh resource entries, one per stream\n    19\t   reflection and one per cursor cell (two waiters sharing one cell\n    20\t   share the one entry, S7.5.3), and wait-set entries that merge the\n    21\t   caller's register context verbatim, so a lowered wait parks under\n    22\t   the engine's own sweep exactly as an unmigrated one does.\n    23\t\n    24\t   Where the engine polls a wait at one of its own fixed resource\n    25\t   keys, the lower parks the wait there: a link wait's pair under\n    26\t   `yin.vm.module`'s link-request-resource and\n    27\t   link-response-resource, reflections and entry fields alike, and a\n    28\t   retained request's response route as the call-out cursor cell the\n    29\t   restored response wait reads (`vm/call-out-cursor-key`), naming\n    30\t   the emitter's own call-out reflection. The resumer's own call pair\n    31\t   under `vm/call-in-stream-key` and `vm/call-out-stream-key` is the\n    32\t   composition's own supply and is never replaced: the outstanding\n    33\t   call routes to the emitter's pair, future calls of the resumed\n    34\t   program to the resumer's own.\n    35\t\n    36\t   `lift-pending` and `lower-pending` are plain-data functions,\n    37\t   parameterized by two composition callbacks rather than coupled to\n    38\t   `yin.vm` or `dao.stream.remote` internals:\n    39\t\n    40\t     serve!  : handle -> {:dao.stream/identity id\n    41\t                          :dao.stream/channel chan} | nil\n    42\t               the exporter's own table entry\n    43\t               (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.3): nil when the handle\n    44\t               cannot be served -- no table to enter it in, no\n    45\t               channel to the resumer -- which refuses the lift as\n    46\t               `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before minting the value\n    47\t               (S7.4.3).\n    48\t\n    49\t     attach! : remote-descriptor -> {:dao.stream/outcome\n    50\t                                     :dao.stream/ok\n    51\t                                     :dao.stream/handle reflection}\n    52\t               | outcome-map\n    53\t               the receiver's own `:dao.stream/attach` dispatch for\n    54\t               `:dao.stream/remote` (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.4): an\n    55\t               outcome other than `ok` -- including a reflection\n    56\t               already marked gone -- refuses the lower as\n    57\t               `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity.\n    58\t\n    59\t   The `:dao.stream.remote/v1` claim is checked, not declared: a cell\n    60\t   is published only after its kept cursor survives a round trip\n    61\t   through the channel codec -- encoded, decoded back, equal -- which\n    62\t   is what plain data in the channel's portable domain means\n    63\t   (S7.4.3; `dao.stream.remote.md` S2.3's plain-data basis).\n    64\t   `cursor-codec` is the default codec; a composition whose channel\n    65\t   speaks another passes its own `{:encode f :decode f}` to\n    66\t   `lift-pending` or `lift-frame`. A cursor that does not round trip\n    67\t   -- a host object inside is the case S2.3 names -- refuses the lift\n    68\t   as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before any value is minted.\n    69\t\n    70\t   Every pending variant of S7.4.3 lifts and lowers: `:next`, `:put`,\n    71\t   `:ffi`, `:ffi-request`, and the linker's `:link-request`,\n    72\t   `:link-response` and `:install` (`yin.vm.linker.md` S7.2-7.3).\n    73\t   `:install` names no stream and passes through unchanged.\n    74\t\n    75\t   Scope: `lift-frame` and `lower-frame` are the frame-level entry\n    76\t   points -- cells are minted and remapped across the WHOLE entry\n    77\t   set, so two waiters on one engine cursor cell lift to one UCF\n    78\t   cell and lower back into one shared cursor entry, and independent\n    79\t   cells stay independent (S7.5.3, and the sharing rule of the S7.11\n    80\t   pending-state blocker). `lift-pending` and `lower-pending` lift or\n    81\t   lower one entry into a self-contained table.\"\n    82\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    83\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n    84\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n    85\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    86\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(def cursor-profiles\n    90\t  \"The one portable cursor profile S7.4.3 names in :yin.k/requires: a\n    91\t   cursor is plain data in the channel's portable domain and the\n    92\t   source honors it after serialization, cursor identity, position and\n    93\t   `gap` outcomes included.\"\n    94\t  #{:dao.stream.remote/v1})\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(def cursor-codec\n    98\t  \"The channel codec a lift round-trips kept cursors through when the\n    99\t   composition supplies none: the DaoStream v2 wire codec, whose\n   100\t   portable domain is the domain the profile claim names.\"\n   101\t  {:encode cbor/encode, :decode cbor/decode})\n   102\t\n   103\t\n   104\t(def ^:private requires\n   105\t  {:yin.k/cursor-profiles cursor-profiles})\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t;; =============================================================================\n   109\t;; Markers (S7.5.1), through a remote descriptor\n   110\t;; =============================================================================\n   111\t\n   112\t(defn- local-identity\n   113\t  \"The identity `h` itself declares, when its descriptor answers ok;\n   114\t   nil otherwise -- the best name a refusal can give when `serve!` has\n   115\t   not minted one.\"\n   116\t  [h]\n   117\t  (let [d (when (stream/descriptor? h) (stream/descriptor h))]\n   118\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome d))\n   119\t      (:dao.stream/identity d))))\n   120\t\n   121\t\n   122\t(defn- unsatisfied\n   123\t  ([] (unsatisfied nil))\n   124\t  ([identity]\n   125\t   (cond-> {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied}\n   126\t     (some? identity) (assoc :dao.stream/identity identity))))\n   127\t\n   128\t\n   129\t(defn- portable-cursor\n   130\t  \"The kept cursor when it survives a round trip through the channel\n   131\t   codec -- encoded, decoded back, and equal -- which is what the\n   132\t   `:dao.stream.remote/v1` claim means: plain data in the channel's\n   133\t   portable domain, honored after serialization. nil when it does\n   134\t   not, so no cell is ever published over an unpublishable cursor.\"\n   135\t  [codec cursor]\n   136\t  (try (let [w (:encode codec)\n   137\t             b (w cursor)\n   138\t             r ((:decode codec) b)]\n   139\t         (when (= cursor r) cursor))\n   140\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\t(defn lift-marker\n   144\t  \"The UCF stream marker (S7.5.1) for local handle `h`, its descriptor\n   145\t   the remote descriptor `serve!` mints by entering `h` in the\n   146\t   exporter's table (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.2). nil when `serve!`\n   147\t   refuses -- the source cannot be entered, or cannot honor the\n   148\t   profile -- so a caller mints `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before any value\n   149\t   is produced (S7.4.3).\"\n   150\t  [serve! h]\n   151\t  (when-let [served (serve! h)]\n   152\t    (let [id (:dao.stream/identity served)]\n   153\t      {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream,\n   154\t       :dao.stream/identity id,\n   155\t       :dao.stream/descriptor\n   156\t       {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote,\n   157\t        :dao.stream/identity id,\n   158\t        :dao.stream/channel (:dao.stream/channel served)}})))\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\t(defn lower-marker\n   162\t  \"The local handle `attach!` gives for UCF stream marker `m` -- a\n   163\t   reflection on the original logical stream -- or nil when the attach\n   164\t   does not answer ok: a reflection marked gone, or any other refusal,\n   165\t   is unsatisfied at the caller.\"\n   166\t  [attach! m]\n   167\t  (let [result (attach! (:dao.stream/descriptor m))]\n   168\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   169\t      (:dao.stream/handle result))))\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\t;; =============================================================================\n   173\t;; The pending wait (S7.4.3)\n   174\t;; =============================================================================\n   175\t;;\n   176\t;; Local pending-entry shapes this namespace lifts, and lowers back to\n   177\t;; -- the engine's own wait-set shapes, plain data resolved through\n   178\t;; the machine's `:resources` table:\n   179\t;;\n   180\t;;   {:reason :next          :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}\n   181\t;;                           :stream-id sid}          ; blocked read\n   182\t;;   {:reason :next          :call-id id :op op        ; a sent call's\n   183\t;;                           :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}\n   184\t;;                           :stream-id sid}          ; response wait\n   185\t;;   {:reason :put           :stream-id sid            ; blocked write,\n   186\t;;                           :datom v}                 ; :datom the retry\n   187\t;;   {:reason :put           :request-sent true        ; a retained FFI\n   188\t;;                           :call-id id :op op        ; request; the\n   189\t;;                           :stream-id sid :datom env}; emitter's pair\n   190\t;;   {:reason :link-request  :link-id id :name m :envelope e\n   191\t;;                           :request rid :response rid' :cursor kept}\n   192\t;;   {:reason :link-response :link-id id :name m\n   193\t;;                           :request rid :response rid' :cursor kept}\n   194\t;;   {:reason :install       :name m}\n   195\t;;\n   196\t;; `cid` names a `:resources` cursor entry `{:stream-id sid :cursor\n   197\t;; kept}` (`vm/cursor-entry`); `sid` and `rid` name stream handles in\n   198\t;; the same table; `env` is the retained `dao.stream.apply` envelope.\n   199\t;; On a real entry the retained request's `sid` is the engine's fixed\n   200\t;; `vm/call-in-stream-key`, and a link wait's `rid`/`rid'` are the\n   201\t;; fixed `module/link-request-resource` and\n   202\t;; `module/link-response-resource` -- the keys the engine's own retry\n   203\t;; and poll paths resolve.\n   204\t;; The registers every parked entry also carries -- :segment :pc :env\n   205\t;; :stack :k -- are the machine's own context: the lift ignores them\n   206\t;; (they travel in the frame's continuation slice, not the pending\n   207\t;; wait), and the lower stamps the caller's context back onto the\n   208\t;; entry it builds.\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t;; =============================================================================\n   212\t;; Cell minting and remapping (S7.5.3), across the whole frame set\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t\n   215\t(defn- mint-cell\n   216\t  \"One mint per distinct cursor cell across the WHOLE frame set:\n   217\t   `found` accumulates the remapping {cell key -> UCF cell id} beside\n   218\t   the cells themselves as entries lift, so two waiters on one\n   219\t   engine cursor cell lift to one UCF cell, and independent cells\n   220\t   stay independent, whatever their positions (S7.5.3). `k` is the\n   221\t   cell's identity -- the engine cursor resource id a `:next` waiter\n   222\t   names, or the response stream and kept cursor a link wait names.\n   223\t   The cell carries the marker `serve!` mints for the cell's stream\n   224\t   and its kept cursor, published only after the cursor survives the\n   225\t   codec round trip; nil -- refuse -- when it does not, or when\n   226\t   `serve!` refuses the stream.\"\n   227\t  [serve! found]\n   228\t  (fn [codec k handle kept]\n   229\t    (or (get-in @found [:of k])\n   230\t        (let [portable (portable-cursor codec kept)\n   231\t              marker (when portable (lift-marker serve! handle))]\n   232\t          (when (and portable marker)\n   233\t            (let [cid (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"c-\" (count (:cells @found))))\n   234\t                  cell {:yin.k/stream marker, :yin.k/position portable}]\n   235\t              (swap! found #(-> % (assoc-in [:of k] cid)\n   236\t                                (assoc-in [:cells cid] cell)))\n   237\t              cid))))))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(defn- cursor-cell\n   241\t  \"The engine cursor cell a `:next` entry waits on: `:cursor-ref`'s\n   242\t   id into `:resources`, the cursor entry carrying the stream id and\n   243\t   the kept cursor (`vm/cursor-entry`). nil when it names nothing.\"\n   244\t  [resources entry]\n   245\t  (let [cid (get-in entry [:cursor-ref :id])\n   246\t        cell (get resources cid)]\n   247\t    (when (and (some? cid) (map? cell) (contains? cell :stream-id))\n   248\t      {:id cid, :stream-id (:stream-id cell), :cursor (:cursor cell)})))\n   249\t\n   250\t\n   251\t(defn- cell-identity\n   252\t  \"The stream identity a `:yin.k/cells` entry names, for a refusal\n   253\t   that names it even when the cell itself never lowered.\"\n   254\t  [cells cell-id]\n   255\t  (get-in cells [cell-id :yin.k/stream :dao.stream/identity]))\n   256\t\n   257\t\n   258\t;; =============================================================================\n   259\t;; Lift: one variant each\n   260\t;; =============================================================================\n   261\t\n   262\t(defn- lift-read\n   263\t  \"S7.4.3's blocked read: the cell `mint` gives for the entry's\n   264\t   cursor cell, at its kept position.\"\n   265\t  [resources mint codec entry]\n   266\t  (if-let [cell (cursor-cell resources entry)]\n   267\t    (if-let [cid (mint codec (:id cell)\n   268\t                       (get resources (:stream-id cell))\n   269\t                       (:cursor cell))]\n   270\t      {:pending {:yin.k/reason :next, :yin.k/cell cid}, :cell? true}\n   271\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity (get resources (:stream-id cell)))))\n   272\t    (unsatisfied)))\n   273\t\n   274\t\n   275\t(defn- lift-response-wait\n   276\t  \"S7.4.3's sent call: a `:next` entry carrying `:call-id`, the\n   277\t   response wait `semantic-restore` rebuilds -- the call's\n   278\t   correlation beside the response cell at its kept position, so\n   279\t   round-order on the cell is preserved on the resumer (S7.4.3).\n   280\t   The call's `:op` rides when the entry carries it (the engine's\n   281\t   own response wait does not; a caller assembling the frame takes\n   282\t   it from the call's parked record).\"\n   283\t  [resources mint codec entry]\n   284\t  (if-let [cell (cursor-cell resources entry)]\n   285\t    (if-let [cid (mint codec (:id cell)\n   286\t                       (get resources (:stream-id cell))\n   287\t                       (:cursor cell))]\n   288\t      {:pending (cond-> {:yin.k/reason :ffi,\n   289\t                         :yin.k/call-id (:call-id entry),\n   290\t                         :yin.k/cell cid}\n   291\t                  (contains? entry :op) (assoc :yin.k/op (:op entry))),\n   292\t       :cell? true}\n   293\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity (get resources (:stream-id cell)))))\n   294\t    (unsatisfied)))\n   295\t\n   296\t\n   297\t(defn- lift-write\n   298\t  \"S7.4.3's blocked write: the target stream's marker and the\n   299\t   retained `:datom` -- the value a retry appends, without which the\n   300\t   wait is not re-establishable. A put names a stream, never a\n   301\t   cursor cell.\"\n   302\t  [serve! resources entry]\n   303\t  (let [h (get resources (:stream-id entry))]\n   304\t    (if-let [marker (lift-marker serve! h)]\n   305\t      {:pending {:yin.k/reason :put,\n   306\t                 :yin.k/stream marker,\n   307\t                 :yin.k/value (:datom entry)}}\n   308\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity h)))))\n   309\t\n   310\t\n   311\t(defn- call-out-cell\n   312\t  \"The emitter's call-out cursor cell -- the response route a retained\n   313\t   request's retry reads through. Once the request lands, the engine\n   314\t   restores the call as a response wait parked on its fixed local\n   315\t   call-out keys (`semantic-restore` -> `response-wait-entry`), so\n   316\t   the route is the cursor entry under `vm/call-out-cursor-key`,\n   317\t   reading the call-out stream under `vm/call-out-stream-key` at its\n   318\t   kept position. nil when the composition holds no call pair, which\n   319\t   no real retained request does -- the pair is checked before the\n   320\t   call parked.\"\n   321\t  [resources]\n   322\t  (let [cursor (get resources vm/call-out-cursor-key)\n   323\t        handle (get resources vm/call-out-stream-key)]\n   324\t    (when (and (map? cursor) (contains? cursor :stream-id) handle)\n   325\t      {:id vm/call-out-cursor-key,\n   326\t       :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key,\n   327\t       :cursor (:cursor cursor)})))\n   328\t\n   329\t\n   330\t(defn- lift-retained-request\n   331\t  \"S7.4.3's retained call: a `:put` entry carrying `:request-sent`,\n   332\t   the request the engine holds unsent. The request stream's marker\n   333\t   is served, and `:request-op` and `:request-args` are read out of\n   334\t   the retained envelope so a resumer rebuilds it verbatim under the\n   335\t   call id. The response route rides beside them as a cell: the\n   336\t   emitter's call-out cursor (`call-out-cell`) at its kept position --\n   337\t   once the retry lands, the response is read on the emitter's\n   338\t   call-out stream through it, so a resumer without the cell strands\n   339\t   the restored response wait. The cell shares by identity like any\n   340\t   other: a response wait on the same call-out cursor lifts to it too\n   341\t   (S7.5.3). `:yin.k/unsatisfied` when the request stream cannot be\n   342\t   served, or the route is missing or its cursor does not survive the\n   343\t   codec round trip.\"\n   344\t  [serve! resources mint codec entry]\n   345\t  (let [h (get resources (:stream-id entry))\n   346\t        envelope (:datom entry)\n   347\t        route (call-out-cell resources)]\n   348\t    (if-let [cid (when route\n   349\t                   (mint codec (:id route)\n   350\t                         (get resources (:stream-id route))\n   351\t                         (:cursor route)))]\n   352\t      (if-let [marker (lift-marker serve! h)]\n   353\t        {:pending {:yin.k/reason :ffi-request,\n   354\t                   :yin.k/call-id (:call-id entry),\n   355\t                   :yin.k/request-op (or (apply2/request-op envelope)\n   356\t                                         (:op entry)),\n   357\t                   :yin.k/request-args (apply2/request-args envelope),\n   358\t                   :yin.k/request marker,\n   359\t                   :yin.k/response-cell cid},\n   360\t         :cell? true}\n   361\t        (unsatisfied (local-identity h)))\n   362\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity\n   363\t                     (or (and route (get resources (:stream-id route)))\n   364\t                         h))))))\n   365\t\n   366\t\n   367\t(defn- lift-link\n   368\t  \"S7.4.3's link variants: the link pair name the engine's own fixed\n   369\t   link keys on a real entry (`module/link-request-resource` and\n   370\t   `module/link-response-resource`), resolved into `:resources`, and\n   371\t   the kept `:cursor` on the response stream\n   372\t   (`module/require-handler` mints it before the envelope is appended)\n   373\t   is the response cell -- a cell like any other, at its kept\n   374\t   position. Both pair markers travel, the engine's `:link-response`\n   375\t   entry retaining its `:request` beside the response side; the\n   376\t   envelope rides verbatim on the request-in-hand variant.\"\n   377\t  [serve! resources mint codec entry]\n   378\t  (let [reason (:reason entry)\n   379\t        resp-h (get resources (:response entry))\n   380\t        req-h (get resources (:request entry))\n   381\t        kept (:cursor entry)]\n   382\t    (if-let [cid (mint codec [(:response entry) kept] resp-h kept)]\n   383\t      (let [resp (lift-marker serve! resp-h)\n   384\t            req (lift-marker serve! req-h)]\n   385\t        (if (and resp req)\n   386\t          {:pending (cond-> {:yin.k/reason reason,\n   387\t                             :yin.k/link-id (:link-id entry),\n   388\t                             :yin.k/name (:name entry),\n   389\t                             :yin.k/request req,\n   390\t                             :yin.k/response resp,\n   391\t                             :yin.k/cell cid}\n   392\t                      (= :link-request reason)\n   393\t                      (assoc :yin.k/envelope (:envelope entry))),\n   394\t           :cell? true}\n   395\t          (unsatisfied (local-identity (or req-h resp-h)))))\n   396\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity resp-h)))))\n   397\t\n   398\t\n   399\t(defn- lift-one\n   400\t  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lifted into `{:pending data,\n   401\t   :cell? bool}` -- `:cell?` true when the variant names a cursor\n   402\t   cell, which is what puts `:dao.stream.remote/v1` in\n   403\t   `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` -- or an outcome carrying\n   404\t   `:yin.k/status`: `:yin.k/unsatisfied` when a stream cannot be\n   405\t   served or a kept cursor does not survive the codec round trip;\n   406\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`, naming the reason, when the entry is no\n   407\t   variant at all.\"\n   408\t  [serve! resources mint codec entry]\n   409\t  (case (:reason entry)\n   410\t    :next\n   411\t    (if (:call-id entry)\n   412\t      (lift-response-wait resources mint codec entry)\n   413\t      (lift-read resources mint codec entry))\n   414\t\n   415\t    :put\n   416\t    (if (:request-sent entry)\n   417\t      (lift-retained-request serve! resources mint codec entry)\n   418\t      (lift-write serve! resources entry))\n   419\t\n   420\t    :link-request (lift-link serve! resources mint codec entry)\n   421\t\n   422\t    :link-response (lift-link serve! resources mint codec entry)\n   423\t\n   424\t    :install\n   425\t    {:pending {:yin.k/reason :install, :yin.k/name (:name entry)}}\n   426\t\n   427\t    {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable, :yin.k/reason (:reason entry)}))\n   428\t\n   429\t\n   430\t;; =============================================================================\n   431\t;; Lift: the frame and the single entry\n   432\t;; =============================================================================\n   433\t\n   434\t(defn lift-frame\n   435\t  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pending entries, lifted together:\n   436\t   `{:yin.k/pending [data ...], :yin.k/cells {cell-id cell},\n   437\t   :yin.k/requires requires}` on success -- the cells minted across\n   438\t   the WHOLE set, so two waiters on one engine cursor cell lift to\n   439\t   one UCF cell (S7.5.3) and `:dao.stream.remote/v1` rides in\n   440\t   `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` when any entry names a cell; the S7.9\n   441\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` outcome, naming the stream identity where one\n   442\t   is known, when any entry's stream cannot be served or its kept\n   443\t   cursor does not survive the codec round trip; the S7.9\n   444\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` outcome when any entry names no variant.\n   445\t   Failure is total (S7.5.4): a refused leaf refuses the frame.\"\n   446\t  ([serve! resources entries]\n   447\t   (lift-frame serve! resources entries cursor-codec))\n   448\t  ([serve! resources entries codec]\n   449\t   (let [found (atom {:cells {}, :of {}})\n   450\t         mint (mint-cell serve! found)\n   451\t         lifted (mapv #(lift-one serve! resources mint codec %) entries)]\n   452\t     (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %) lifted)]\n   453\t       refusal\n   454\t       {:yin.k/pending (mapv :pending lifted),\n   455\t        :yin.k/cells (:cells @found),\n   456\t        :yin.k/requires (if (some :cell? lifted) requires {})}))))\n   457\t\n   458\t\n   459\t(defn lift-pending\n   460\t  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lifted into its own self-contained\n   461\t   cell table: `{:yin.k/pending data, :yin.k/cells {cell-id cell},\n   462\t   :yin.k/requires requires}` on success, with the same `:yin.k/\n   463\t   unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable` outcomes `lift-frame`\n   464\t   gives. Frame assembly that shares cells across entries is\n   465\t   `lift-frame`'s.\"\n   466\t  ([serve! resources entry]\n   467\t   (lift-pending serve! resources entry cursor-codec))\n   468\t  ([serve! resources entry codec]\n   469\t   (let [r (lift-frame serve! resources [entry] codec)]\n   470\t     (if (contains? r :yin.k/status)\n   471\t       r\n   472\t       (assoc r :yin.k/pending (first (:yin.k/pending r)))))))\n   473\t\n   474\t\n   475\t;; =============================================================================\n   476\t;; Lower: back into resources and a wait-set entry\n   477\t;; =============================================================================\n   478\t\n   479\t(defn- stream-id-of\n   480\t  \"The fresh resource id the reflection of the stream `identity`\n   481\t   names stands under: derived from the identity, so two markers of\n   482\t   one stream attach once and lower shared. A receiver composing\n   483\t   these entries into a live `:resources` table re-keys them -- the\n   484\t   ids are fresh for the lowered state, the remap mechanical.\"\n   485\t  [identity]\n   486\t  (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"s-\" identity)))\n   487\t\n   488\t\n   489\t(defn- pending-markers\n   490\t  \"Every stream marker `pending` names beside its cells' -- the\n   491\t   target of a put, the request of a retained call, the pair of a\n   492\t   link request.\"\n   493\t  [pending]\n   494\t  (case (:yin.k/reason pending)\n   495\t    :put [(:yin.k/stream pending)]\n   496\t    :ffi-request [(:yin.k/request pending)]\n   497\t    (:link-request :link-response)\n   498\t    [(:yin.k/request pending)\n   499\t     (:yin.k/response pending)]\n   500\t    []))\n   501\t\n   502\t\n   503\t(defn- attach-all\n   504\t  \"One attach per distinct stream identity, each ok installing its\n   505\t   reflection under `stream-id-of`'s fresh resource id. The first\n   506\t   attach that does not answer ok -- a reflection marked gone\n   507\t   included -- refuses the lower as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming that\n   508\t   identity.\"\n   509\t  [attach! markers]\n   510\t  (let [by-id (reduce (fn [m mkr]\n   511\t                        (update m (:dao.stream/identity mkr) #(or % mkr)))\n   512\t                      {} markers)]\n   513\t    (reduce-kv (fn [acc identity mkr]\n   514\t                 (if-let [h (lower-marker attach! mkr)]\n   515\t                   (let [sid (stream-id-of identity)]\n   516\t                     (-> acc\n   517\t                         (assoc-in [:streams identity] sid)\n   518\t                         (assoc-in [:resources sid] h)))\n   519\t                   (reduced (unsatisfied identity))))\n   520\t               {:streams {}, :resources {}}\n   521\t               by-id)))\n   522\t\n   523\t\n   524\t(defn- cell-resources\n   525\t  \"The fresh cursor entry each UCF cell lowers to: its carried\n   526\t   position, on the reflection of the stream the cell names, under\n   527\t   the cell id itself -- two references to one cell share the one\n   528\t   entry, and one ref per cell keeps its own (S7.5.3). A\n   529\t   response-route cell lowers under the engine's fixed\n   530\t   `vm/call-out-cursor-key` instead, the one key the restored\n   531\t   response wait reads (`semantic-restore`'s own shape), still naming\n   532\t   the emitter's own call-out reflection: the outstanding call routes\n   533\t   to the emitter's pair, while the resumer's own pair under the\n   534\t   fixed stream keys is never replaced.\"\n   535\t  [streams cells routes]\n   536\t  (into {}\n   537\t        (map (fn [[cid cell]]\n   538\t               [(if (contains? routes cid)\n   539\t                  vm/call-out-cursor-key\n   540\t                  cid)\n   541\t                {:stream-id (streams (cell-identity cells cid)),\n   542\t                 :cursor (:yin.k/position cell)}]))\n   543\t        cells))\n   544\t\n   545\t\n   546\t(defn- route-cells\n   547\t  \"The UCF cell ids a frame's retained requests name as their\n   548\t   response route: the cells that lower under the engine's fixed\n   549\t   call-out cursor key rather than their own id, so the route's\n   550\t   readers -- the restored response wait, a co-waiting `:ffi`\n   551\t   response wait -- share the one entry (S7.5.3).\"\n   552\t  [pendings]\n   553\t  (into #{} (keep :yin.k/response-cell) pendings))\n   554\t\n   555\t\n   556\t(defn- cursor-entry-key\n   557\t  \"The resource id `cid`'s cursor entry lowers under: the cell id\n   558\t   itself, except a response-route cell -- it lowers under the\n   559\t   engine's fixed `vm/call-out-cursor-key`, the key the restored\n   560\t   response wait reads (`semantic-restore`'s own shape).\"\n   561\t  [routes cid]\n   562\t  (if (contains? routes cid) vm/call-out-cursor-key cid))\n   563\t\n   564\t\n   565\t(defn- link-pair-resources\n   566\t  \"The engine's fixed link keys (`module/link-request-resource`,\n   567\t   `module/link-response-resource`) mapped onto the reflections a\n   568\t   lowered link wait uses: the engine's sweep retries a\n   569\t   `:link-request` by appending at the fixed request key\n   570\t   (`module/append-link-request`) and polls a `:link-response` at the\n   571\t   fixed response key (`poll-link-response`), so a lowered link wait\n   572\t   parks where the engine looks. One pair per task: the identity of\n   573\t   each side rides the pending's own markers.\"\n   574\t  [streams resources pendings]\n   575\t  (reduce (fn [acc pending]\n   576\t            (if (contains? #{:link-request :link-response}\n   577\t                           (:yin.k/reason pending))\n   578\t              (let [req-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n   579\t                                            :dao.stream/identity])\n   580\t                    resp-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/response\n   581\t                                             :dao.stream/identity])\n   582\t                    req (get resources (streams req-id))\n   583\t                    resp (get resources (streams resp-id))]\n   584\t                (if (and req resp)\n   585\t                  (assoc acc\n   586\t                         module/link-request-resource req,\n   587\t                         module/link-response-resource resp)\n   588\t                  acc))\n   589\t              acc))\n   590\t          {}\n   591\t          pendings))\n   592\t\n   593\t\n   594\t(defn- lower-one\n   595\t  \"One pending of S7.4.3, lowered: `{:entry e}` -- the wait-set\n   596\t   entry the receiver parks, the caller's register context `ctx`\n   597\t   merged verbatim under the stream and cell fields the data\n   598\t   rebuilds -- or an outcome carrying `:yin.k/status`, naming the\n   599\t   stream identity where one is known. `routes` are the frame's\n   600\t   response-route cells (`route-cells`): a wait on one reads the\n   601\t   engine's fixed call-out cursor.\"\n   602\t  [pending ctx streams cells routes]\n   603\t  (case (:yin.k/reason pending)\n   604\t    :next\n   605\t    (let [cid (:yin.k/cell pending)]\n   606\t      (if-let [sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))]\n   607\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   608\t                       {:reason :next,\n   609\t                        :stream-id sid,\n   610\t                        :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref,\n   611\t                                     :id (cursor-entry-key routes cid)}})}\n   612\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   613\t\n   614\t    :put\n   615\t    (if-let [sid (streams (get-in pending [:yin.k/stream\n   616\t                                           :dao.stream/identity]))]\n   617\t      {:entry (merge ctx\n   618\t                     {:reason :put,\n   619\t                      :stream-id sid,\n   620\t                      :datom (:yin.k/value pending)})}\n   621\t      (unsatisfied (get-in pending [:yin.k/stream :dao.stream/identity])))\n   622\t\n   623\t    :ffi\n   624\t    (let [cid (:yin.k/cell pending)\n   625\t          cursor-id (cursor-entry-key routes cid)]\n   626\t      (if-let [sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))]\n   627\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   628\t                       (cond-> {:reason :next,\n   629\t                                :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n   630\t                                :stream-id sid,\n   631\t                                :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref,\n   632\t                                             :id cursor-id}}\n   633\t                         (contains? pending :yin.k/op)\n   634\t                         (assoc :op (:yin.k/op pending))))}\n   635\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   636\t\n   637\t    :ffi-request\n   638\t    (let [req-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n   639\t                                  :dao.stream/identity])\n   640\t          route-id (cell-identity cells (:yin.k/response-cell\n   641\t                                          pending))]\n   642\t      (if (and (streams req-id) (streams route-id))\n   643\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   644\t                       {:reason :put,\n   645\t                        :request-sent true,\n   646\t                        :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n   647\t                        :op (:yin.k/request-op pending),\n   648\t                        :stream-id (streams req-id),\n   649\t                        :datom (apply2/request (:yin.k/call-id pending)\n   650\t                                               (:yin.k/request-op\n   651\t                                                 pending)\n   652\t                                               (:yin.k/request-args\n   653\t                                                 pending))})}\n   654\t        (unsatisfied (if (streams req-id) route-id req-id))))\n   655\t\n   656\t    (:link-request :link-response)\n   657\t    (let [reason (:yin.k/reason pending)\n   658\t          cid (:yin.k/cell pending)\n   659\t          sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))\n   660\t          req (streams (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n   661\t                                        :dao.stream/identity]))]\n   662\t      (if (and sid req)\n   663\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   664\t                       (cond-> {:reason reason,\n   665\t                                :link-id (:yin.k/link-id pending),\n   666\t                                :name (:yin.k/name pending),\n   667\t                                :request module/link-request-resource,\n   668\t                                :response module/link-response-resource,\n   669\t                                :cursor (:yin.k/position (get cells cid))}\n   670\t                         (= :link-request reason)\n   671\t                         (assoc :envelope (:yin.k/envelope pending))))}\n   672\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   673\t\n   674\t    :install\n   675\t    {:entry (merge ctx\n   676\t                   {:reason :install, :name (:yin.k/name pending)})}\n   677\t\n   678\t    {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable,\n   679\t     :yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)}))\n   680\t\n   681\t\n   682\t(defn lower-frame\n   683\t  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pendings, lowered together:\n   684\t   `{:entries [e ...], :resources table}` -- one fresh stream entry\n   685\t   per distinct served identity and one fresh cursor entry per\n   686\t   distinct UCF cell, two waiters on one cell lowering back into one\n   687\t   shared entry and independent cells staying independent (S7.5.3) --\n   688\t   with the same `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   689\t   outcomes `lift-frame` gives. Where the engine resolves a wait at\n   690\t   one of its own fixed resource keys, the lowered reflections stand\n   691\t   under exactly those keys: a link wait's pair under\n   692\t   `module/link-request-resource` and `module/link-response-resource`,\n   693\t   a retained request's route cell as the fixed call-out cursor\n   694\t   entry, naming the emitter's own call-out reflection. The resumer's\n   695\t   own call pair under the fixed stream keys is never replaced --\n   696\t   outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair, future calls to the\n   697\t   resumer's own. `contexts`, parallel to `pendings`, is each\n   698\t   entry's register context, merged verbatim.\"\n   699\t  [attach! pendings cells contexts]\n   700\t  (let [ms (distinct (remove nil?\n   701\t                             (concat (map :yin.k/stream (vals cells))\n   702\t                                     (mapcat pending-markers pendings))))\n   703\t        a (attach-all attach! ms)]\n   704\t    (if (contains? a :yin.k/status)\n   705\t      a\n   706\t      (let [routes (route-cells pendings)\n   707\t            lowered (mapv #(lower-one %1 %2 (:streams a) cells routes)\n   708\t                          pendings contexts)]\n   709\t        (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %)\n   710\t                               lowered)]\n   711\t          refusal\n   712\t          {:entries (mapv :entry lowered),\n   713\t           :resources (merge (:resources a)\n   714\t                             (cell-resources (:streams a) cells routes)\n   715\t                             (link-pair-resources (:streams a)\n   716\t                                                  (:resources a)\n   717\t                                                  pendings))})))))\n   718\t\n   719\t\n   720\t(defn lower-pending\n   721\t  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lowered: `{:entry e, :resources\n   722\t   table}` -- the wait-set entry `ctx`'s registers ride on, and the\n   723\t   fresh resource entries it polls through -- with the same\n   724\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable` outcomes\n   725\t   `lower-frame` gives. Frame assembly that shares cursor entries\n   726\t   across entries is `lower-frame`'s.\"\n   727\t  [attach! pending cells ctx]\n   728\t  (let [r (lower-frame attach! [pending] cells [ctx])]\n   729\t    (if (contains? r :yin.k/status)\n   730\t      r\n   731\t      (-> r\n   732\t          (assoc :entry (first (:entries r)))\n   733\t          (dissoc :entries)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '545,630p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '545,630p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"   545\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n   546\t\n   547\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   548\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   549\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   550\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\n   551\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n   552\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\n   553\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n   554\t\n   555\t```clojure\n   556\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n   557\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\n   558\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n   559\t\n   560\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n   561\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\n   562\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n   563\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n   564\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n   565\t\n   566\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n   567\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n   568\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n   569\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\n   570\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n   571\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n   572\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n   573\t\n   574\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n   575\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n   576\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n   577\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n   578\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n   579\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n   580\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n   581\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n   582\t```\n   583\t\n   584\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n   585\t\n   586\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   587\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   588\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   589\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   590\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   591\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   592\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   593\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   594\t  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\n   595\t  cell through `dao.stream.remote.md`: the exporter enters the cell's\n   596\t  handle in its table and the cell's stream descriptor becomes a remote\n   597\t  descriptor. This is an access path to the original logical stream, not\n   598\t  a copied stream with new positions. `dao.stream` itself assumes no\n   599\t  network. The mirror serves the source's declared reader and writer\n   600\t  surface, preserving its outcome maps and cursor namespace rather than\n   601\t  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n   602\t  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\n   603\t  cursor. `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` names\n   604\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, the one profile: a cursor is plain data in the\n   605\t  channel's portable domain and the source honors it after\n   606\t  serialization, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n   607\t  If the source cannot be entered in a table, preserve the cursor, or\n   608\t  keep a channel reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n   609\t  minting the value. A reflection marked gone on the receiver is also\n   610\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity. The exporter keeps\n   611\t  the entry served under a lease the resumer holds\n   612\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6). Re-homing a stream is out of\n   613\t  scope: serving from the exporter pins the exporter.\n   614\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   615\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   616\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   617\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   618\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   619\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   620\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   621\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   622\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   623\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   624\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   625\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   626\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   627\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   628\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   629\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   630\t  value), where through a reflection `ok` is acceptance on the outbound\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n    19\t   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    34\t\n    35\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    36\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    37\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    38\t\n    39\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Streams\n    42\t\n    43\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    44\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    45\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    46\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    47\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    48\t\n    49\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    50\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    51\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    52\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    53\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    54\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    55\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    56\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    57\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    58\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    59\tthe stream's own.\n    60\t\n    61\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    62\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    63\t\n    64\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    65\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    66\t\n    67\t### Host Boundaries\n    68\t\n    69\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    70\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    71\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    74\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    75\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    76\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    77\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    78\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    79\t\n    80\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    81\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    82\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    83\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    84\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    85\t\n    86\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    87\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    88\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    89\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    90\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    91\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    92\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    93\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    94\tnot an upward callback.\n    95\t\n    96\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    97\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    98\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    99\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n   100\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n   101\tfor facts it owns.\n   102\t\n   103\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   104\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   105\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   106\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   107\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   108\t\n   109\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   110\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n   111\thappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n   112\tevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n   113\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   114\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   115\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   116\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   117\t\n   118\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   119\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   120\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   121\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   122\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   123\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Architectural Commitments\n   126\t\n   127\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n   128\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n   129\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n   130\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
