{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '510,550p;1305,1385p;1415,1442p'\"","aggregated_output":"   510\t  \"The engine's composition for `dao.stream.waitset/check`: the whole\n   511\t   resolver contract in one value, both functions synchronous and pure over\n   512\t   the immutable resources table. Neither touches a transport -- the\n   513\t   library polls, the engine maps.\n   514\t\n   515\t   `:resolve` maps one whole parked entry to its live handle plus the\n   516\t   cursor to read from -- or the value to append -- out of the VM's\n   517\t   `:resources`. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an\n   518\t   earlier round baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same\n   519\t   cursor-ref may have advanced the cell since. `:advance` commits a woken\n   520\t   reader's successor -- or recovery -- cursor to its cell before later\n   521\t   entries resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances within the round.\"\n   522\t  {:resolve (fn [resources entry]\n   523\t              (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n   524\t                (let [cursor-data (get resources (:id cursor-ref))]\n   525\t                  {:stream (or (:stream entry)\n   526\t                               (get resources (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n   527\t                   :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)})\n   528\t                {:stream (or (:stream entry) (get resources (:stream-id entry)))\n   529\t                 :value (:datom entry)}))\n   530\t   :advance (fn [resources entry cursor]\n   531\t              (let [cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))]\n   532\t                (if cursor-id\n   533\t                  (assoc-in resources [cursor-id :cursor] cursor)\n   534\t                  resources)))})\n   535\t\n   536\t\n   537\t;; =============================================================================\n   538\t;; Lift and lower: the portable encoding a linked slice crosses in\n   539\t;; (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, acts 1 and 4; UCF section 7.5.1)\n   540\t;; =============================================================================\n   541\t\n   542\t(defn- non-portable!\n   543\t  [kind data]\n   544\t  (fail \"Value is not portable\"\n   545\t        (merge data {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable, :yin.k/kind kind})))\n   546\t\n   547\t\n   548\t(defn- scalar?\n   549\t  [x]\n   550\t  (or (nil? x) (boolean? x) (number? x) (string? x) (keyword? x)\n  1305\t            (refuse-install state module-name\n  1306\t                            {:status :lost, :reason reason}))\n  1307\t          state\n  1308\t          (keys (:installs state))))\n  1309\t\n  1310\t\n  1311\t(defn check-wait-set\n  1312\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n  1313\t\n  1314\t   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n  1315\t   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n  1316\t   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n  1317\t   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n  1318\t   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n  1319\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n  1320\t\n  1321\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n  1322\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n  1323\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n  1324\t   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n  1325\t   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n  1326\t   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n  1327\t   resource-id form.\n  1328\t\n  1329\t   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n  1330\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n  1331\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n  1332\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n  1333\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n  1334\t\n  1335\t   The link wait entries `require` adds (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.2) are\n  1336\t   polled first, by the engine rather than the stream sweep: a\n  1337\t   `:link-request` retries its envelope, a `:link-response` advances its\n  1338\t   own kept cursor to the response carrying its id, and an `:install`\n  1339\t   waits on its install child. The install children are then stepped in\n  1340\t   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n  1341\t   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\"\n  1342\t  [state]\n  1343\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n  1344\t    (if (and (empty? wait-set) (empty? (:installs state)))\n  1345\t      state\n  1346\t      (let [state (poll-links state\n  1347\t                              (filterv link-entry? wait-set)\n  1348\t                              (remove link-entry? wait-set))\n  1349\t            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n  1350\t            streams (filterv (complement link-entry?) wait-set)\n  1351\t            {:keys [woken store], :as result}\n  1352\t            (waitset/check {:waiting streams}\n  1353\t                           waitset-resolver\n  1354\t                           (:resources state))\n  1355\t            v (assoc state\n  1356\t                     :resources store\n  1357\t                     :wait-set (into (:waiting (:waitset result))\n  1358\t                                     (filter link-entry?)\n  1359\t                                     wait-set))]\n  1360\t        (update v\n  1361\t                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1362\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n  1363\t\n  1364\t\n  1365\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n  1366\t\n  1367\t\n  1368\t(defn run-loop\n  1369\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n  1370\t   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n  1371\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n  1372\t  (loop [v state]\n  1373\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n  1374\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n  1375\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n  1376\t                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n  1377\t                              (recur resumed)\n  1378\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n  1379\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n  1380\t                      (recur resumed)\n  1381\t                      v)\n  1382\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n  1383\t\n  1384\t\n  1385\t(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n  1415\t\n  1416\t\n  1417\t(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n  1418\t  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n  1419\t   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n  1420\t   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  1421\t  [entry o]\n  1422\t  (cond\n  1423\t    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n  1424\t    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n  1425\t    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n  1426\t    (= :link-refused o)\n  1427\t    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n  1428\t               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n  1429\t          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n  1430\t    (= :next (:reason entry))\n  1431\t    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n  1432\t          {:outcome o,\n  1433\t           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n  1434\t           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n  1435\t    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n  1436\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n  1437\t\n  1438\t\n  1439\t(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  1440\t  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n  1441\t   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n  1442\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '885,920p;1090,1120p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | sed -n '260,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '885,920p;1090,1120p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | sed -n '260,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"   885\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   886\t\n   887\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   888\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   889\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   890\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   891\t\n   892\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   893\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   894\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   895\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   896\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   897\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   898\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   899\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   900\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   901\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   902\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   903\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   904\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   905\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   906\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   907\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   908\t\n   909\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   910\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   911\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   912\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   913\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   914\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   915\t\n   916\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   917\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   918\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   919\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   920\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n  1090\thandles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private `:resources`\n  1091\ttable in VM state, beside the store, never inside it. The engine's own\n  1092\tmachinery -- wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume --\n  1093\treads and writes only that table, and no user store instruction and no\n  1094\t`resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module closure.\n  1095\tThe ids may stay predictable: they name nothing a store instruction can\n  1096\treach. A migration slice still carries resource cells, remapped at resume,\n  1097\tbut beside the program store; the decode targets of sections 7.5.1 and\n  1098\t7.5.3 move with them.\n  1099\t\n  1100\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n  1101\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n  1102\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n  1103\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n  1104\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n  1105\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n  1106\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n  1107\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n  1108\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n  1109\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n  1110\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n  1111\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n  1112\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n  1113\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n  1114\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n  1115\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n  1116\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n  1117\taccident.\n  1118\t\n  1119\t**There is no merge at the module grain either (linker M4).** Specified by\n  1120\t`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. A module install's exports cross\n   260\t   with full and the entry keeps its envelope -- a retained\n   261\t   `:link-request`, the engine's own fields, registers and pair.\"\n   262\t  [name]\n   263\t  (let [request (one-slot-stream \"link-request\")\n   264\t        response (ring 64)\n   265\t        _ (stream/append! request :warmed)\n   266\t        datoms (requirer-datoms name)\n   267\t        parked (vm/run (load-ast (linking-machine request response)\n   268\t                                 datoms))\n   269\t        entry (first (:wait-set parked))]\n   270\t    [datoms parked entry request response]))\n   271\t\n   272\t\n   273\t(defn- call-node\n   274\t  \"A `:dao.stream.apply/call` of `op` with one literal argument.\"\n   275\t  [op arg]\n   276\t  {:type :dao.stream.apply/call, :op op,\n   277\t   :operands [(lit arg)]})\n   278\t\n   279\t\n   280\t(defn- then\n   281\t  \"`((fn [_] second) first)`: first for its effect, then second.\"\n   282\t  [first-ast second-ast]\n   283\t  {:type :application,\n   284\t   :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_], :body second-ast},\n   285\t   :operands [first-ast]})\n   286\t\n   287\t\n   288\t(defn- calling-machine\n   289\t  \"A fresh semantic machine composed over an FFI call pair, under the\n   290\t   engine's own fixed pair keys (`vm.cljc`'s `:call-in`/`:call-out`\n   291\t   composition keys).\"\n   292\t  [call-in call-out]\n   293\t  (semantic/create-vm\n   294\t    {:make-stream make-ring-stream,\n   295\t     :capability-secret secret,\n   296\t     :call-in call-in,\n   297\t     :call-out call-out}))\n   298\t\n   299\t\n   300\t(defn- parked-caller\n   301\t  \"A real machine parked on a retained FFI request: the call-in\n   302\t   stream holds one warm-up value, so the first call's put refuses\n   303\t   with full and the entry keeps the request -- the engine's own\n   304\t   `:request-sent` shape, the pair under its fixed resource keys.\n   305\t   `ast` may call again after the first call, so a resume can watch\n   306\t   where a future call routes.\"\n   307\t  [ast]\n   308\t  (let [call-in (one-slot-stream \"call-in\")\n   309\t        call-out (ring 8)\n   310\t        _ (stream/append! call-in :warmed)\n   311\t        datoms (vm/ast->datoms ast)\n   312\t        parked (vm/run (load-ast (calling-machine call-in call-out)\n   313\t                                 datoms))\n   314\t        entry (first (:wait-set parked))]\n   315\t    [datoms parked entry call-in call-out]))\n   316\t\n   317\t\n   318\t(defn- registers-of\n   319\t  \"The register payload a parked entry carries.\"\n   320\t  [entry]\n   321\t  (select-keys entry [:segment :pc :env :stack :k]))\n   322\t\n   323\t\n   324\t(defn- quiesced-receiver\n   325\t  \"The fresh engine state a lower installs into: a real machine over\n   326\t   the same code image, quiesced, holding the lowered resource table\n   327\t   and the lowered wait-set entry -- nothing of the emitter's state\n   328\t   but the code image and the registers the entry carries.\"\n   329\t  ([datoms lowered] (quiesced-receiver datoms lowered (new-machine)))\n   330\t  ([datoms lowered machine]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
